The volunteers who open the hall need the new rota and a
one-page key handover. If you send loose files, someone may
download one and miss the other. If you send a bundle, you
need to know what's in it and how to tell whether the
download matches what you sent. Those are separate tasks:
packing, inspecting, restoring and comparing.

\begin{objectives}
\item Distinguish an archive from compression and from a backup plan.
\item Create, list and restore a small, trusted archive without overwriting existing files.
\item Compare SHA-256 digests and explain what a match does and does not establish.
\end{objectives}
\index{tar@\texttt{tar}}
\index{gzip@\texttt{gzip}}
\index{archive}
\index{checksum}

\section{Pack named files together}
Open a spare terminal tab and start a separate \cmd|sh| session.
The setup exits that practice shell on failure, so it cannot
continue writing in the directory where you started. The
non-POSIX \cmd|mktemp -d| creates a fresh directory; the six
Xs in the template mark its generated suffix. The \cmd|case|
check accepts only a path with the expected prefix and six
suffix characters. Then the bracketed test requires \cmd|pwd -L|
to match the captured path exactly before any fixture writes.
The logical path keeps the \texttt{/tmp} spelling even where
it is a symbolic link. These are deliberately synthetic
documents: don't replace them with actual keys, key codes,
names or personal contact details.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp23.XXXXXX) || exit 1
case "$practice" in
  /tmp/utp23.??????) ;;
  *) printf '%s\n' 'Unexpected practice path.' >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
cd "$practice" || exit 1
[ "$(pwd -L)" = "$practice" ] || exit 1
mkdir handover || exit 1
printf '%s\n' 'Friday: early shift' > handover/rota.txt || exit 1
printf '%s\n' 'Return the practice key to the desk.' \
  > handover/key-note.txt || exit 1
\end{terminal}

If the setup exits or reports an error, stop and investigate
instead of running the next block at your usual prompt.

An \FirstUse{archive} is a file containing a representation of
other files and often their paths and metadata. In this example
it holds two files and their parent directory. The tool is
\cmd|tar|, and the new archive will be \texttt{handover.tar}.
Use relative operands so that members can be restored beneath
a directory of your choice.

\begin{terminal}
tar -cf handover.tar handover
tar -tf handover.tar
\end{terminal}

In the first command, \cmd|-c| creates the archive; \cmd|-f|
makes the following argument its filename. That name is not a
source. The last operand, \texttt{handover}, is the source
directory, and its contents are included. If \texttt{handover.tar}
already exists, creation can overwrite it. In this freshly
created directory the name is unused. On real data choose an
unused destination outside the tree you are packing; putting
the output inside the input tree risks collecting an unfinished
archive or otherwise producing a misleading result.

In the second command, \cmd|-t| lists members; it doesn't
restore them. Expect \texttt{handover/} and the two file names
beneath it, not necessarily in the same order on every system.
Compare the inventory with what you meant to send. If an archive
contains an entire directory rather than an explicit file list,
hidden files may be present too. You cannot infer from the one
directory operand that the resulting archive includes only the
two visible files you remember seeing. A path like a saved
settings file is enough reason to inspect before distributing.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-23/layers.tikz}
\caption{Archiving groups names and contents; compression encodes the resulting bytes.}
\label{fig:23-layers}
\VisualDescription{Two files and their directory become one tar archive, and gzip can then encode that archive into one compressed file.}
\end{figure}

\section{Compress without replacing the archive}
Compression represents a byte stream differently so it often
takes fewer bytes to store or send. It doesn't make a member
list, and it is not encryption (Figure~\ref{fig:23-layers}). Here \cmd|gzip -c| writes
compressed bytes to standard output and leaves the input file
in place. Redirect the output to a fresh name. Don't write
the result over the source or run the command if either of the
two paths is not what you expect.

\begin{terminal}
gzip -c handover.tar > handover.tar.gz
gzip -t handover.tar.gz
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-t| asks gzip to test the compressed stream for integrity;
success normally produces no text and status 0. It doesn't
prove the contents are appropriate or restore correctly.
Compression may shrink a tar archive of short text because
the tar format has regular structure and padding. Some image
and video formats are already compressed, so mixing them into
an archive and compressing again may save little. Don't
estimate the download size from an extension. Compare the
actual sizes:

\begin{terminal}
wc -c handover.tar handover.tar.gz
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-c| orders \cmd|wc| to count bytes. Each line pairs the
count with a filename, and a total line sums the two inputs.
For judging which file to send, use the two individual
counts rather than the total. A small size says nothing
about whether the right files were packed.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.29in}Y}
\toprule
Operation & Question it answers \\
\midrule
\texttt{tar -tf} & What are the member names in this tar archive? \\
\texttt{gzip -t} & Does the compressed stream pass gzip's integrity check? \\
\texttt{wc -c} & How many bytes would I store or transfer for each version? \\
SHA-256 & Does this byte sequence match a trusted fingerprint? \\
Restore drill & Can I retrieve the files I meant to save? \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Restore in a fresh destination}
\label{sec:23-restore}
Practice only on the archive you made here. Listing members
helps catch an unexpected source or destination, but it is
not proof that an archive from an unfamiliar sender is safe
to extract. Potential trouble includes absolute paths, a
\texttt{..} component, special files, links, multiple entries
for the same path and very large decompressed data. Tools and
versions handle these differently. Don't extract an unfamiliar
archive merely because the visible names look ordinary, and
never extract as an administrator for this exercise. A new
destination helps prevent ordinary collisions; it is not a
security boundary for untrusted content.

First produce another plain archive from the compressed
practice file. Use a distinct unused filename. With
\cmd|gzip -dc|, \cmd|-d| selects decompression and \cmd|-c|
keeps the compressed original while writing the result to
standard output. Stop if the command fails; a shell can
leave an empty or partial output file even after failure.
Then compare the uncompressed bytes to the original.

\begin{terminal}
gzip -dc handover.tar.gz > restored.tar &&
  cmp handover.tar restored.tar &&
  tar -tf restored.tar
\end{terminal}

Each \cmd|&&| runs what follows only if the preceding step
succeeds. Expect the byte comparison to be silent (status 0)
and the list to show the same three members: \texttt{handover/},
\texttt{handover/rota.txt} and \texttt{handover/key-note.txt},
in any order. If the comparison or listing fails, or the
inventory disagrees in any respect, stop; do not run the
extraction. This is a comparison of the
exact plain archives, not a claim that creating a fresh tar
at a different time will always produce identical bytes.
Metadata, order and format choices can change even if
the documents' text is unchanged.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir recovered &&
  tar -xf restored.tar -C recovered &&
  cmp handover/rota.txt recovered/handover/rota.txt &&
  cmp handover/key-note.txt recovered/handover/key-note.txt
\end{terminal}

Stop if \cmd|mkdir| fails. \cmd|-x| extracts; \cmd|-C|
tells the tar implementations used here to change into the
named directory before extracting. Because the member paths
start with \texttt{handover/}, that directory appears
\emph{inside} \texttt{recovered} (Figure~\ref{fig:23-restore}). The original is still next
to the archive. The two comparisons should return status 0.
This is a restore drill: inspect the reconstructed files,
not only the name of the archive or the size of its output.
It checks these two file contents, not ownership, permissions
or all possible metadata.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-23/restore.tikz}
\caption{Extract beneath the fresh destination, then compare with the source.}
\label{fig:23-restore}
\VisualDescription{Original files remain under handover. An extracted second handover tree appears under recovered, making it possible to compare the two.}
\end{figure}

\section{Check the exact file you intend to transfer}
A digest is a fixed-length fingerprint calculated from a
byte sequence. SHA-256 writes 256 bits, conventionally shown
as 64 hexadecimal characters. Matching digests provide strong
evidence that two byte sequences are the same if you trust
both the expected digest and the tool. A mismatch establishes
that the previously fingerprinted bytes and the currently
fingerprinted bytes differ, assuming the records used the
same algorithm and are accurate. Neither comparison explains
\emph{why} a change happened.

Select the utility your system actually has. These commands
show the file digest in differing formats. Use only one row,
not one of each, for the exercises. \cmd|command -v| can
show whether a named command is available; consult local
manuals when names or options differ.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.15in}Y}
\toprule
Common tool & Command and reading \\
\midrule
GNU Coreutils & \texttt{sha256sum handover.tar.gz}; the hex digest precedes the filename. \\
macOS Perl wrapper & \texttt{shasum -a 256 handover.tar.gz}; the algorithm option selects SHA-256. \\
BSD alternatives & Use \texttt{sha256 -q handover.tar.gz} where available, or OpenBSD \texttt{cksum -a sha256 -q handover.tar.gz}. Here \texttt{-q} shows only the digest. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

On a system with GNU Coreutils, make a mock second copy of
the compressed archive and compare the digests:

\begin{terminal}
cp -i handover.tar.gz copy.tar.gz
sha256sum handover.tar.gz copy.tar.gz
\end{terminal}

Other systems can run the chosen digest command separately on
each file. The displayed filenames differ but the 64 hex
digits match. The same output from two files on one disk says
nothing about resilience to loss of that disk. If you attach
a digest to a real delivery, the recipient should recalculate
it after downloading the file and compare against the expected
value (Figure~\ref{fig:23-verify}). For adversarial tampering, an expected digest delivered
through the same untrusted channel as the file does not
authenticate either one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-23/verify.tikz}
\caption{Calculate both fingerprints over the same representation.}
\label{fig:23-verify}
\VisualDescription{One digest is made from the compressed file before transfer and one from the received compressed file. Compare the digests, not the file labels.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{What did the check establish?}
\Brian{The checksum matches. Does that mean the archive is safe to open?}
\Stephen{It means the bytes match what the expected checksum describes.}
\Brian{If the sender packed the wrong folder, the checksum could still match.}
\Stephen{Yes. It doesn't know the intended contents.}
\Brian{And if someone changes both the file and the checksum?}
\Stephen{Matching them won't catch that. You need a trusted source for the expected value.}
\Brian{So I'll verify both the source and the files I can actually restore.}
\DialogueConclusion{A digest compares bytes, not intentions.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{platformnote}{Linux, macOS and the BSDs}
\texttt{tar} is widely available but its interface is not
specified by POSIX; POSIX defines the \texttt{pax} utility.
GNU tar and implementations used by macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD
and NetBSD offer the basic create, list and extract modes.
Do not assume that all versions accept GNU long options
or preserve the same extended metadata. Gzip and SHA-256
utility names and options are likewise outside these POSIX
interfaces. Consult \texttt{man tar} and the applicable
digest manual on the destination machine.
\end{platformnote}

\section{Rehearse the handoff, not just the packing}
Think through what the recipient can do without calling you.
They need to identify the exact artifact, obtain the expected
fingerprint by an appropriate channel, inspect what they got,
restore it somewhere they can write and open the files.
The filenames \texttt{handover.tar} and
\texttt{handover.tar.gz} describe two different byte
sequences. If someone compares the compressed download
against a digest of the decompressed file, the mismatch
doesn't necessarily point to damaged data; it can point to
a mismatch in instructions. Put both the filename and
algorithm beside a shared digest, and record whether it
applies before or after decompression.

There is a useful failure test you can do without modifying
an archive at all. Calculate one digest for the archive and
one for just the rota. Then pretend the recipient accidentally
picked the rota when comparing against the archive's digest.
The file chosen is valid and the command works, but the pair
of bytes is not the intended pair.

\begin{terminal}
sha256sum handover.tar.gz handover/rota.txt
\end{terminal}

Use the equivalent command from the table if necessary. Read
the two labels along with the different digests. In real
work, if the fingerprint doesn't match, stop before using
the delivery. Check the algorithm, filename, version and
source of the expected fingerprint. If they all agree,
obtain a clean copy and compare again. Do not edit the received
file in an attempt to make a comparison pass, or dismiss a
mismatch as harmless because a familiar title is visible.
Re-downloading won't help if the sender repeatedly selects
the wrong source artifact, which is why useful instructions
name what the file \emph{should} contain.

\subsection{Choose what to pack on purpose}
The original exercise gave tar a whole directory because the
directory was new and every file was part of the handover.
For a real deliverable, an explicit member list can be a
better boundary. An editor might leave a hidden autosave
alongside a finished file. A note may be safe to keep
locally but inappropriate to distribute. An explicit list
also gives the recipient a short inventory against which
to check their copy. It makes omissions easier to see
than a long listing does.

When deciding what metadata to carry, distinguish sharing
documents from preserving a filesystem. This exercise checks
the two restored files byte for byte, not whether an
owner or timestamp was restored. Tar archives can carry
mode bits, ownership information and times; factors such
as implementation, options, permissions and the
destination filesystem affect what can be restored.
Some systems have additional metadata that is not handled
uniformly. If a job requires that information, define the
requirement and test representative files on the destination
before discarding the source. Do not change permissions or
switch identities just to make the toy exercise look like
a preservation test.

If space is tight, remember that listing and restoring are
different operations. The exercise temporarily uses room
for both the compressed and restored tar files, and later
for the expanded document tree. The compressed byte count
alone cannot tell you whether the expanded data will fit.
If a restore runs out of space, assume the destination is
partial. Keep the source archive and don't merge partial
results with a working folder. Verify available room and start
again in a distinct empty destination rather than hoping a
second extraction will finish what the first one started.

\subsection{Check the members you actually sent}
When packing a set of files, there can be a gap between
the list you pass as operands and the list in the bundle.
A named directory can grow between the time you inspect
it and the time tar reads it. If a source file is being
actively saved while tar runs, the bytes captured may not
represent the settled version you expected. For an ordinary
document handoff, close the editor after saving and use a
small explicit list of final files. Don't rely on packing a
live, changing tree as a snapshot of everything at one instant.

An extended member listing can help you check more than
the names. Here \cmd|-v| requests a verbose listing.
It still doesn't extract. Output formatting and which
metadata fields appear vary by implementation.

\begin{terminal}
tar -tvf handover.tar
\end{terminal}

Look for the directory and two ordinary file entries.
The path at the end of each entry identifies the member;
the preceding parts can show a type and permission string,
owner, size and modification time. A directory's displayed
size isn't the sum of the files inside it. The archive
also may not contain the names printed by your file browser
exactly as shown there; for example, a directory member
commonly ends with a slash. This listing can disclose
metadata you didn't intend to share, such as account names.
If that matters, review a known-safe publication process
before sending. The exercise has no real data to redact.

The next step is to open the recovered text and confirm its
meaning. Byte equality with a mistaken original only
reproduces the mistake accurately. In a handover, someone
who wasn't involved in creating the bundle can often spot
such problems: the note says Friday, for example, but the
instructions say to use the bundle on Saturday. You can
verify byte integrity mechanically while still asking a
person to verify that the information is the right one.

\subsection{An archive is one ingredient in a backup}
The word ``backup'' describes an ability to recover, not
a filename ending. This archive and its local copy are
both on the same storage in the exercise. They can help
practice comparisons but cannot save you from the loss
of that storage. A good backup arrangement distinguishes
the original, independent copies and the versions you are
prepared to recover. It also makes sure the people who
need the recovery procedure can find it. For an extended
restore plan, see Chapter~\ref{ch:41} after this practice.

Reading one restored copy that matches today's source is
already a better check than admiring a progress bar. It
still doesn't tell you whether an older version is recoverable
or whether another device can access the bundle. Set the
scope of each check in plain words: ``I recovered these
two files from this archive into a fresh directory and
their contents matched.'' That is useful, testable evidence
and does not promise more than you actually tested.

